1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760
Not every wisdom tooth needs to come out - but the difference matters, so check this before you decide anything.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,280
Dentists used to pull them out as a precaution just in case.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:16,720
Current guidance says that's not automatic anymore: if there's no infection and no damage to the teeth around it, removal isn't necessary.

4
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:19,280
So what actually forces the decision?

5
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:25,920
A flap of gum tissue often covers part of an emerging wisdom tooth, and food and bacteria get trapped underneath it.

6
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:34,400
That's pericoronitis - throbbing pain, bad breath, and a gum that looks red and puffy, sometimes bad enough that opening your mouth gets hard.

7
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:36,160
There's a second trigger too.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:40,880
When the tooth comes in tilted instead of upright, it leans on the molar right next to it.

9
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:46,400
That constant pressure can wear down the neighboring tooth's root and open up a gap where cavities form.

10
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:55,920
Either one of those - infection that keeps returning, or damage to the tooth beside it - is what tips the decision toward extraction, and it's simplest in your early twenties.

11
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,520
No infection, a clean bite, easy to brush around?

12
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,600
Regular dental checkups are enough.

13
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:09,120
While you're figuring out which one you're dealing with: warm salt water rinses and a cold compress for 20 minutes at a time take the edge off.

14
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:17,280
But if the pain sticks around past a day or two, or you're having trouble opening your mouth or swallowing, that's your sign to get seen - don't just ride it out.
